<!--
  Flood labelling screen for Label Studio.
  Paste it into the project's Settings → Labeling Interface → Code.

  It is the equivalent of CLASSES, CLASS_COLORS and TOOLS in flood/label.py: it
  declares the 4 classes with their colours and which tools exist. It computes
  nothing.

  The colours are for SEEING what you draw, so they are chosen against the
  background each class gets drawn on, not by resemblance to the thing. cloud is
  dark red and not white: the region fill is semi-transparent, and white over a
  white cloud cannot be told apart while you are drawing it. That is also why
  dry_land is earth and not grey, which is the colour of the SAR.

  The 3 tools share a single <Labels>, so they are picked from the toolbar as
  convenient:
    wand     click on the water and drag to widen the tolerance. Fastest when the
             patch is dark and uniform (SAR in dB).
    brush    paint and erase by hand. For touching up what the wand left.
    polygon  vertex by vertex. For when the threshold does not catch.

  Wand and brush return RLE masks; the polygon returns vertices.

  radar_shadow applies only to SAR and cloud only to optical. The type of each
  scene travels in the task (image_type, set by make_tasks.py) and is visible in
  the header.

  The PNG can carry several views of the same place stitched side by side,
  separated by a magenta stripe. It is ONE image as far as Label Studio is
  concerned, so this screen does not change according to how many views the scene
  has. Draw on whichever panel shows it best: import_labels.py discounts the
  panel offset and everything lands on the same place on the map. What you should
  NOT do is cross the stripe with a stroke.

  When hosting, if the PNGs are served from another domain, the <Image> needs
  crossOrigin="anonymous" and that domain has to send CORS headers: the wand reads
  the pixels in the browser and without that it does not work.
-->
<View>

  <Header value="$place_id — $image_type — $view_label"/>

  <Labels name="label" toName="img">
    <Label value="water"        background="#0a84ff"/>
    <Label value="dry_land"     background="#c69c6d"/>
    <Label value="cloud"        background="#d70015"/>
    <Label value="radar_shadow" background="#bf5af2"/>
  </Labels>

  <MagicWand name="wand"  toName="img" defaultthreshold="15" blurradius="5"/>
  <Brush     name="brush" toName="img"/>
  <Polygon   name="poly"  toName="img" strokeWidth="2"/>

  <Image name="img" value="$image"
         zoom="true" zoomControl="true"
         brightnessControl="true" contrastControl="true"/>

</View>
